 % \subsubsection{Read-Modify-Write Instructions} UV3 needs a read-modify-write (RMW) instruction rather than a plain store. CleanupSpec assumes that a store requests its cache line only when it commits~\cite[\S 4]{saileshwar2019cleanupspec}, so a speculative store never changes the cache, and, as in our other specifications, we do not model speculative stores as transmitters. An x86 RMW such as \texttt{or \$0, (mem)} is different: its load half issues speculatively and requests the line with write permission, and CleanupSpec keeps no record with which to undo that request. Every RMW transmitter our model admits is a load transmitter with the load relabelled (an Alloy check finds all 150 minimal RMW cores of up to four instructions to be relabelled load cores), so we derive an RMW corpus from the load corpus by replacing each transmitter load with an RMW to the same address (124{,}198 tests). One more condition matters: our tests zero their memory before the speculation window, so the RMW's line is already cached and there is nothing to undo. When the test's memory is flushed after that setup, CleanupSpec leaves the RMW's line installed in 407 of 590 sampled tests, against 0 of 590 without the flush. The other defenses protect an RMW's load half like a plain load.
