 \subsection{Timing And CPU Usage}\label{sec:timing}
 We measure cost as wall-clock time on equal numbers of cores. Each \toolname{} test is one single-threaded gem5 run at AMuLeT's configuration for the defense, so its wall time equals its CPU time. Generating a test takes an estimated 0.93 CPU-seconds and running it 1.3 to 1.7, by defense; both tools' per-test costs are measured on the same idle node, each keeping about 16 hardware threads busy. Counting generation, on the same number of cores \toolname{} detects A-UV1, A-UV4 and A-UV6 $4.9$--$53\times$ faster than AMuLeT, while AMuLeT detects A-UV3 about $1.2\times$ faster and the rare A-UV5 about $31\times$ faster (Fig.~\ref{fig:timedet}). Generation is a one-time cost, and the generated tests are reused when a defense is patched; AMuLeT generates programs inside its fuzzing loop, so its time per detection already includes generation.
